  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/068eeaf4f0ac63aafca9ee09f06b94f6e52cbe00b4a625970881fe105e299a40.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=a+\\lambda b,$ find $\\lambda .$",
  "worked_solution": "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13931259/assets/45a4735bf7710ff7c2699a9b42b10c34807d6162a3d7da38842ba87f13158788.png)\n\nNote that in $\u25b3OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{1}{2}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nSince $a\u2226b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$",
